    new "Andrei Chikatilo — a Soviet serial killer and cannibal. He sexually assaulted and killed at least forty-three people, mostly women and children. He was later deemed legally sane and sentenced to death by a firing squad."


    old "Кар-Мэн - советская и российская технопоп-группа начала и середины девяностых. Была основана Богданом Титомиром и Сергеем Лемохом. Запомнилась экстравагантными танцами и музыкальной теме к мультфильму \"Капитан Пронин\"."
    new "Car-Man – a Soviet and Russian technopop band most popular from the late 80s to early 90s, founde by Bogdan Titomir and Sergey Lemokh. It is mostly remembered for unique dancing performances and for writing a music theme for the \"Captain Pronin\" cartoon."


translate english strings:


    old "Лапта - русская народная командная игра с мячом и битой. Цель игры — ударом биты послать мяч в поле, перебежать поле до противоположной стороны и вернуться обратно, не дав противнику «осалить» себя мячом. К родственным лаптe видам спорта относятся бейсбол и крикет."
    new "Lapta— traditional Russian team sport played with a bat and a ball. The goal of the game is to bat the ball into the field, cross the field to the other side and return back without letting the opponent hit you with said ball. This places Lapta in the same family of sports as baseball and cricket."


    old "Чебуреки - блюдо крымскотатарской кухни, представляет собой кулинарное изделие в виде полукруга из пресного теста с начинкой из измельчённой жирной баранины, лука, специй и небольшого количества воды. Обжаривается во фритюре."
    new "Chebureki — a dish from Crimean Tatar cuisine. It's made by frying a folded semicircle of unleavened dough filled with minced lamb meat with lard, onion, spices, and a small amount of water."


    old "Стенька Разин - один из самых известных казачьих атаманов в российской истории. По легендам, он и его подручные зарыли в русской земле целые состояния с десятками пудов золота и жемчуга."
    new "Sten'ka Razin — one of the most famous cossack commanders in Russian history. According to legends, he and his subordinates had buried huge amounts of treasure with tens of pound of pearls and gold all over the country."


    old "До шестнадцати и старше - советская и российская телевизионная передача, посвящённая проблемам молодёжи: беспризорность, движение «рокеров», темы наркомании и «дедовщины», проблемы досуга и взаимоотношений в семье."
    new "Under Sixteen And Beyond — a Soviet and Russian TV program covering the problems of 90s youths: homelessness, the \"rock\" movement, substance abuse, troubles in familial relationships, and lack of healthy hobbies. "


    old "Николай Дроздов - советский и российский учёный-зоолог, путешественник, популяризатор науки и ведущий телепередачи «В мире животных». Обладает заразительным оптимизмом и трогательным отношением ко всем животным."
    new "Nikolai Drozdov — a Soviet and Russian zoologist, traveler, popular science advocate and a TV host featuring in the \"Inside the Animal Kingdom\" program. He is famous for his infectuous optimism and overwhelmingly empathetic attitude toward animals."


    old "Элен и ребята - французский молодёжный телесериал, показанный, помимо Франции, в Европе и многих странах Восточной Европы в середине 1990-х годов."
    new "Helen and the Boys — a French TV series targeted at teenagers that was broadcasted all over Europe, including most Eastern European countries in the middle of the 1990s."


    old "Давид Ойстрах - один из наиболее известных представителей русской скрипичной школы. Активно выступал в качестве дирижёра, завоевав в этой области имя мастера. Умер от сердечного приступа через несколько часов после концерта."
    new "David Oistrakh — one of the most prominent Russian violinists. He was also well known for his musical conductor skills, earning the rank of master in that field. Died from a heartstroke hours after one of his concerts."



translate english strings:


    old "{#ExoticTerm_DedMoroz}"
    new "Grandpa Frost (Ded Moroz) —  a legendary figure similar to Santa Claus, hailing from Slavic mythology. He is often depicted wearing a  heel-length fur coat, in red or blue, a semi-round fur hat, and a pir of Russian traditional winter footwear — valenki.  His face is adorned by a long white beard, and he walks with a long magic stick. He's said to bring presents to well-behaving children on the New Year's eve. "


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Zefir}"
    new "Zefir — a type of soft confectionery popular in post-Soviet countries. It's made by whipping fruit and berry purée with sugar and egg whites with subsequent addition of a gelling agent. Its chocolate-coated variant shares the same family of treats with American Mallomars, German Schokokuss, and Iranian Bastani zemestani."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Halva}"
    new "Halva — a confectionery that originated from Persia, usually consisting of a thick paste made from flour, butter, liquid oil, saffron, rosewater, milk, cocoa powder, and sweetened with sugar."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Zayats}"
    new "Zayats, volk... — Khariton explains the etimology of words for \"bunny\" and \"wolf\" in Russian.  The English translation uses transrcribed versions of those words to ensure the comparison to other languages in this line makes sense phonetically."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Honey}"
    new "\"One who oversees the honey\" — a pun based on the two syllables that make up the Russian word for \"bear\", one of which means \"honey\" while the other means \"to have knowledge of something, to oversee something.\""


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Bear}"
    new "\"Here comes the bear...\" — this excerpt is from an old Russian folk tale that tells the story of a bear that steals turnips from a village. During one of his thefts, the bear gets attacked by an old peasant who chops off one of the bear's hind legs and gives it to his wife to make a soup from the meat and cloth from the wool. The bear later returns to the village with a fake hind leg to exact his revenge on the family only to fall into the cellar inside their house and get killed by the vilalgers."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_LifeLine}"
    new "Life line - one of the most significant signs in the practice of palmistry, a divinatory art that interprets the distinctive features of each hand (such as lines and mounts) to obtain information about a person's destiny and personality. Palmistry gained wide popularity among Russian youth in the 80s and 90s, influenced in part by the strong presence of the gypsy culture in the region, from which this art originated."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Rebra}"
    new ""



translate english strings:


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Matryoshka}"
    new "Matryoshka dolls — a set of wooden dolls of decreasing size placed one inside another. A set of matryoshkas consists of a wooden figure, which separates at the middle, top from bottom, to reveal a smaller figure of the same sort inside, which has, in turn, another figure inside of it, and so on. Usually, the outer layer is a woman dressed in a traditional Russian dress, but overall, matryoshkas can be of any gender."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_BabaYaga}"
    new "Baba Yaga — a female character (or one of a trio of sisters of the same name) from Slavic folklore. In some narratives, she is described as a repulsive or ferocious-looking old woman who fries and eats children, while in others she is depicted as a nice old woman who helps the hero. Her distinctive traits are flying around in a wooden mortar, wielding a pestle, and dwelling deep in the forest in a hut with chicken legs."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Naere}"
    new "Naere buzarlalga — a multi-use swear word in the Buryat language, akin to \"Fuck!\" in English or \"Blyat\" in Russian."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Sportlotto}"
    new "Sportlotto — a popular lottery brand in Russia that dates back to the 1970s USSR and was initially designed by the USSR's Sports Committee, hence the name."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_KingAndJester}"
    new "King and Jester — a now disbanded Russian horror punk band. The lyrics of their songs depict horror stories and folk tales featuring many fantasy creatures from Slavic folklore. Despite the macabre themes, many songs incorporate humor, sarcasm, and irony.  King and Jester disbanded a year after its frontman, Mikhail Gorshenev, died from alcohol abuse-induced heart failure."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_NaNa}"
    new "Na-Na — a Russian boy band that was popular in Russia in the 90s. In 2000, they were involved in the \"Planetary anthem\" project from Warner Bros. and Heineken, where they were supposed to record a song on a space station, but after Fredy Heineken died in 2002, the project was discontinued."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Gozinaki}"
    new "Gozinaki — a traditional Georgian brittle confection made of caramelized nuts, usually walnuts, and fried in honey."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Smak}"
    new "Smak — a Russian culinary entertainment television program that debuted in 1993. Some of its episodes featured prominent foreign guests like Chuck Norris and Til Schweiger."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Utrennik}"
    new "Utrennik — a type of entertainment event for kindergarten and primary school kids, featuring stage plays, contests, and other activities."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Vinegret}"
    new "Vinegret — a salad in Russian cuisine which is also popular in other post-Soviet states. The dish includes diced cooked vegetables (red beets, potatoes, carrots), chopped onions, as well as sauerkraut and/or brined pickles."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Raskolnikov}"
    new "Raskolnikov — the protagonist of Dostoyevsky's \"Crime and Punishment\". In the story, he uses an axe to kill an elderly pawnbroker."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Draft}"
    new "Draft-dodging — military service in Russia is mandatory, but several loopholes can be used to dodge the draft. One of them is faking a mental illness or bribing medical staff to admit a person into a psych ward where they have to undergo \"treatment\" and, therefore, are deemed unfit for military service."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Wild90s}"
    new "Wild Nineties — a term with a negative connotation used by Russians to refer to the transitional period after the collapse of the USSR, characterized by financial, political, and social instability."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Kisel}"
    new "Kisel — a simple dish with the consistency of a thick gel, and made of sweet fruit, berries, grains (oatmeal, rye, wheat), peas, or milk."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_LittleBlueLight}"
    new "Little Blue Light — a popular musical variety show that aired on Soviet television since 1962 during various holidays. The show was slowly phased out of TV broadcasts by 1988, but was brought back after an almost ten-year-long hiatus in 1998. In the modern day, it's aired every year on New Year's Eve, right after the president's address."


    old "{#ExoticTerm_Kolobok}"
    new "\"Sweep the flour from the bins\" — this exchange between Alisa and Anton references a Russian short story for children \"Kolobok\" (Little Bun). In it, a childless couple of elderly peasants uses the last grains of flour they have to bake themselves a son who could help around the house. The titular character, however, escapes from them and decides to travel the land. He then meets a variety of animals—a hare, a wolf, and a bear—and evades being eaten by singing them a song. In the end, he meets a cunning fox, and she tricks him into jumping onto her snout before eating him."


    old "Turbo (Турбо) — жевательная резинка с фруктовым вкусом и ароматом, производимая турецкой компанией «Kent Gida», содержащая вкладыши с фотографиями, как правило, автомобилей или мотоциклов. Была чрезвычайно популярна среди детей и подростков позднего СССР, стран СНГ, Польши, Румынии в конце 1980-х — начале 1990-х годов."
    new "Turbo — a brand of chewing gum with fruity flavors, produced by a Turkish company Kent Gida, containing inserts with photos of cars and motorbikes. It was very popular among small kids and teens in late 80s-early 90s USSR, CIS countries, Poland, and Romania."
